<h1>The 8 dimensions that define a workspace that performs for people</h1>
<p class="meta">Spaces That Matter — Blog &amp; Resources</p>
<p class="lead">Thermal comfort, acoustics, natural light, air quality — but also social connections, meaning, governance and local roots. A closer look at each of the 8 dimensions of the Spaces That Matter framework.</p>
<p>How can you tell whether a workspace truly performs from a human point of view? This question, which may seem simple, is in fact one of the hardest to put into practice. It requires drawing simultaneously on neuroscience, environmental psychology, behavioral design and the social sciences.</p>
<p>That is what Spaces That Matter has done, in partnership with La Fabrique Spinoza, by building a framework grounded in more than 200 academic publications. The result: 8 dimensions that together capture what makes a workspace perform for people — or not.</p>

<h2>Dimension 1 — Physical and sensory well-being</h2>
<p>This is the most intuitive dimension — and often the most poorly assessed. Physical and sensory well-being covers everything that directly affects the body and the senses: acoustic quality (background noise, reverberation, speech intelligibility), thermal comfort (temperature, humidity, air circulation), light quality (natural light, color temperature, glare) and indoor air quality (CO₂, humidity, VOCs).</p>
<p>What the studies show: a CO₂ level above 1,000 ppm reduces cognitive capacity by 15 to 30%. A poor acoustic environment increases errors and fatigue. Conversely, good natural light improves sleep quality and reduces absenteeism.</p>

<h2>Dimension 2 — Psychological well-being</h2>
<p>Beyond physical comfort, space deeply affects people's psychological state. This dimension captures the sense of safety, autonomy and control over one's immediate environment. It also includes the notion of personal space — the perception of having a territory of one's own, even in a shared office.</p>
<p>Research on “territoriality at the office” shows that people who have access to a space they can personalize — even just a plant or a personal object — report greater well-being and higher job satisfaction. Flex office, if it is not accompanied by careful thinking about how people make the space their own, can severely degrade this dimension.</p>

<h2>Dimension 3 — Vitality and energy</h2>
<p>A good workspace does more than avoid wearing people out. It regenerates them. This dimension measures the space's ability to restore the mental and physical energy of the people who use it.</p>
<p>So-called “restorative” spaces are characterized by four properties (Kaplan &amp; Kaplan, 1989): symbolic or physical distance from everyday routine, a rich and fascinating environment, a coherent and expansive setting, and compatibility with the individual's needs. Biophilic spaces — with plants, natural light and views of the outdoors — score particularly well on this dimension.</p>

<h2>Dimension 4 — Connections and social vitality</h2>
<p>Space is a powerful regulator of social interaction. Depending on its layout, it can encourage or inhibit informal exchanges, spontaneous collaboration and moments of conviviality. This dimension assesses the quality of the social connections the space helps create and sustain.</p>
<p>One of the most counter-intuitive findings of recent research (Bernstein &amp; Turban, 2018, HBR): open-plan offices, supposed to encourage collaboration, actually reduce face-to-face interactions by 70% — in favor of digital exchanges (email, instant messaging). Physical space must therefore be designed to actively encourage real encounters.</p>
<p>“Spontaneous collaboration doesn't happen by chance — it is designed.”</p>

<h2>Dimension 5 — Meaning and identity</h2>
<p>Workspaces are also spaces of meaning. They embody a culture, values and an identity. A space consistent with the organization's mission and values strengthens employees' sense of belonging and pride. Conversely, a generic, interchangeable space can signal — unintentionally — that the organization does not value its own teams.</p>
<p>This dimension, often overlooked in technical assessments, is nonetheless one of the most discriminating for organizations with a strong culture or a clearly stated purpose.</p>

<h2>Dimension 6 — Fairness and inclusion</h2>
<p>A high-performing space is one that is accessible and suited to everyone. This dimension assesses the extent to which the space meets the needs of people in diverse situations: mobility impairments, sensory hypersensitivities, needs for concentration versus stimulation, specific personal circumstances (breastfeeding, hearing impairment, etc.).</p>
<p>Inclusion in the design of spaces is not just a matter of regulatory compliance: it is a strong marker of an organization's culture, and a lever for retaining talent.</p>

<h2>Dimension 7 — Governance and participation</h2>
<p>How are decisions about the space made? To what extent are employees consulted, involved and informed? This dimension assesses the quality of the governance process for spaces.</p>
<p>Research on organizational change consistently shows that buy-in to a new work environment is strongly correlated with team participation in its design. A co-created space will be better received than an imposed one, even if the latter is objectively of higher quality.</p>

<h2>Dimension 8 — Openness and local roots</h2>
<p>Finally, the eighth dimension assesses the relationship between the workspace and its wider context: its neighborhood, its local area, its ecosystem. A space that is open to the outside, connected to the life of its neighborhood and accessible to partners and the local community creates a dynamic of openness that enriches employees' experience.</p>
<p>At a time when meaning at work is a central concern, this dimension of local roots is becoming increasingly important — especially for younger generations, who expect their workplace to contribute positively to its surroundings.</p>

<h2>Why these 8 dimensions, and not others?</h2>
<p>The choice of these dimensions is not arbitrary. It is the result of an in-depth literature review and consultations with experts in neuroscience, psychology, design, sociology and management.</p>
<p>These 8 dimensions share three characteristics: they are scientifically grounded — each one rests on a solid body of research; they are measurable — through a rigorous employee questionnaire; they are actionable — their results make it possible to identify concrete areas for improvement.</p>
<p>Together, they form a complete and nuanced portrait of what a workspace does to the people who use it. And it is this portrait that Spaces That Matter puts in the hands of the organizations that choose to be certified.</p>
